    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d375">

                <pb facs="0388" n="376" sameAs="frus:frus1939v01/pg376"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">760C.62/1043: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Bullitt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-08-26T00:00:00+01:00">August 26,
                            1939—midnight</date>.<lb/>[Received August 26—8:04 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1613. Bonnet asked me to call on him this evening and said that Coulondre had
                    telegraphed briefly from Berlin that when he had presented to Hitler today
                    Daladier’s reply (see my 1610, August 26, 6 p.m.) to the remarks that Hitler had
                    made yesterday to Coulondre <pb facs="0389" n="377" xml:id="pg377"/> and had
                    stated that Daladier would be most happy if he could play the role of
                    conciliator to bring about direct conversations between Poland and Germany
                    Hitler had replied that he could not accept this method of procedure.</p>
                <p>There is much suspicion in Paris tonight that Chamberlain and Henderson have been
                    engaged today in preparing a careful betrayal of Poland using a variation of the
                    technique that they employed so successfully on Czechoslovakia. I have been
                    unable to find any fact to support this interpretation of the deliberations of
                    the British Government.</p>
                <p>Bonnet stated to me this evening that Corbin<anchor n="74" xml:id="d375-f1"/> had
                    telephoned to him that the British Government was continuing to maintain an
                    absolutely stiff attitude.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bullitt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
